# Switching the morning run for the gym this winter? Here's why Melbourne CBD professionals end up at the osteopath

Monday is a public holiday. It's the first proper long weekend since footy finals talk took over the office, and the gyms around Bourke Street and Collins Street are about to get busy in a way they haven't been since New Year.

A good chunk of that crowd will be people who haven't picked up a barbell since last winter.

Here's the pattern MOSIC's practitioners see every year about this time. Melbourne's mornings turn properly cold, the run along the Tan or the Yarra trail gets a lot less appealing at 6am, and CBD professionals who have spent autumn pounding the pavement decide it's time to move the session indoors. By Tuesday, several of them are walking gingerly into the office, or ringing the Bourke Street clinic to book in.

Swapping running for resistance training through winter is sound thinking. Doing it without easing in, on a body that has spent the working week hunched over a desk near Flinders Lane, is where it goes wrong.

## Why the first cold-weather gym session goes wrong

The body adapts to whatever you ask of it most often. Someone who runs three or four times a week has legs and lungs built for repetitive, low-load movement. Ask that same body to squat, deadlift or bench press, and a different set of muscles, joints and movement patterns suddenly have to do the work.

Add a Melbourne winter to the mix and the risk climbs again. Walking into a gym on Lonsdale Street after a cold commute from Flinders Street station means cold, tight muscles meeting an unfamiliar load, often with the same weights someone was lifting twelve months ago, before a year of desk work and outdoor running quietly changed their strength and mobility without them noticing.

Hip flexors tighten from long stretches of sitting, hamstrings shorten from months of running on concrete without much stretching either side of it, and shoulders round forward from hours over a keyboard each day. None of that shows up on an easy weekend run. It shows up the moment someone tries to hinge at the hips for a deadlift, or press a loaded bar overhead for the first time in months.

Then there's the long weekend itself. Three or four days off work often turns into three or four sessions squeezed into a short stretch, instead of the usual one or two spread across a full week. That sudden jump in training load, landing on a body that wasn't warmed up or conditioned for it, is a common reason MOSIC sees a run of new patients in the days after a long weekend.

## How osteopathic treatment gets you back to training

When someone walks into the Bourke Street or Essendon clinic with a strained lower back or a grumbling shoulder from a weekend at the gym, the assessment doesn't stop at the sore spot.

MOSIC's practitioners look at the whole chain of movement that led to the injury. For a lower back strain from a deadlift, that might mean checking hip mobility, hamstring length, how well the thoracic spine rotates, and whether someone can actually hinge at the hips properly, rather than pressing on the painful muscle and calling the job done.

Treatment is hands-on. It can include joint mobilisation, soft tissue work on the muscles that have tightened up to guard the injured area, and clear guidance on which movements to ease back into and which to leave alone for a week or two. The aim is to settle the pain down and work out why the movement broke down in the first place, so the same strain doesn't resurface the next time training ramps up.

For CBD professionals who can't easily slip away from the desk during the week, telehealth appointments are available for follow-up consultations and exercise reviews, alongside hands-on treatment at either clinic. It's a practical option for anyone trying to fit recovery around a full diary.

