## Three things to do before your next session

A long weekend is a good chance to reset your training, not wreck it. A few small adjustments are usually the difference between walking into work on Tuesday feeling capable, and wincing every time you reach for the kettle.

Start lighter than you remember being able to lift. Strength built up over years of training fades faster than most people expect once it's replaced by months of running and sitting at a desk. The first session back should feel almost too easy. The second and third can build from there, gradually, rather than chasing last year's numbers in week one.

Warm up properly, especially on a cold morning. Five to ten minutes of movement, leg swings, band pull-aparts, bodyweight squats, walking lunges, before touching a weight makes a real difference to how the session feels at the time and how the body responds the next day. Skipping this step on a cold Melbourne morning is asking for trouble.

Pay attention to the parts of the day that have nothing to do with training. Hips that are tight from eight hours at a desk on Collins Street, or shoulders that have rounded forward over a laptop all week, won't magically loosen up the moment you walk into a gym. Standing up, stretching and moving through the working day matters just as much as the session itself.

And if something doesn't settle within a couple of days, get it looked at properly. A tight calf or a niggling lower back that lingers past 48 hours is far easier to sort early than after it's been worked around, favoured and quietly made worse for six weeks.

