King's Birthday weekend lands at exactly the point in the calendar where this pattern peaks. The AFL finals race is heating up, which means weekend footballers are also lifting their own training intensity right as their usual outdoor running routine starts losing its appeal to the cold.

Add a public holiday Monday, and what would normally be one gym session becomes three or four, often on a body that has spent the past few months quietly adjusting to shorter days, colder mornings and longer hours indoors.

MOSIC sees this pattern from both ends of the city. The Bourke Street clinic, close to Parliament station, picks up the CBD commuter crowd switching from the Tan to a Collins Street gym floor. The Essendon clinic, not far from Keilor Road, sees the same thing from the northwest suburbs, where footy training and weekend gym sessions tend to ramp up together as finals season draws closer.

## Don't let a long weekend turn into a long layoff

A long weekend off work is meant to leave you feeling better than when it started, not booking time off later for a sore back. Building load gradually, warming up properly, and paying attention to the niggles that don't settle is usually the difference between a break that resets your training and one that sets it back by months.

If switching from running to the gym this winter has already left you stiff, sore or favouring one side, MOSIC's practitioners can help you work out why and get you back to full training with a clear plan, not just a quick fix that wears off by Friday.

