## Frequently asked questions

**Can I see an osteopath in Melbourne CBD without a referral?**

Yes. You don't need a referral from a GP to book an appointment with an osteopath at MOSIC's Bourke Street clinic or the Essendon clinic. You can book directly online at melbourneosteopathycentre.com.au or call 03 9663 6202. If your visit relates to a work injury or a WorkCover claim, it's worth mentioning this when you book, so the team can walk you through any paperwork that applies. Telehealth appointments are also available if getting to the clinic during business hours is tricky.

**What's the difference between an osteopath and a physio for a gym injury?**

Both professions treat musculoskeletal injuries and there's real overlap in what they do day to day. Osteopaths tend to take a broader view of the body, looking at how different joints, muscles and movement patterns connect and contribute to an injury, rather than focusing only on the painful spot. For a gym strain, an osteopath might assess your hips, spine and shoulders together to work out why the injury happened, then combine hands-on treatment with guidance on rebuilding strength safely. Many people choose based on what's worked for them before.

**How many sessions will I need for a strained lower back?**

It depends on how long you've had the issue, how it started, and how your body responds to treatment. A recent strain from one heavy session might settle within two or three appointments, alongside some changes to how you train in the meantime. A back that's been niggling on and off for months usually takes longer, since there's often a movement pattern or a build-up of tightness behind it that needs addressing as well. Your practitioner will give you a clearer picture after the first assessment.

**Can a tight lower back cause hip or leg pain?**

Yes, and it's quite common. The lower back, hips and legs share muscles, nerves and movement patterns, so tightness or irritation in one area often shows up somewhere else entirely. A tight lower back can refer pain into the buttock or the back of the thigh, and it can change the way you walk, run or lift, which then puts extra strain on the hips and knees. This is part of why MOSIC's practitioners assess the whole lower body rather than just the spot where the pain is felt.

**Is a gym or sport injury covered by WorkCover, private health or Medicare?**

It depends on how and where the injury happened. If it occurred while you were at work or carrying out work duties, it may fall under WorkCover, and MOSIC's team can talk you through what that involves. Most private health funds with extras cover include a rebate for osteopathy, so it's worth checking your level of cover before your visit. Medicare rebates for osteopathy are limited and generally require a GP management plan, so the clinic's reception staff can help clarify what applies to your situation before you book.

